1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:04,320
It is July 17th, 1996, Gate 27,

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:06,260
Kennedy Airport, New York.

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,880
A group of French club students from a small Pennsylvania town board,

4
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:11,960
a wide bodyjet, bound for Paris.

5
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:15,920
The flight is running more than an hour behind schedule,

6
00:00:16,340 --> 00:00:17,780
a broken piece of ground equipment,

7
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:19,300
a bag that needs pulling.

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,220
Routine. Nobody on board has any reason to think it matters.

9
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:28,520
At 8 .19 that evening, TWA flight 800 lifts off,

10
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:30,880
climbing out over Long Island toward the Atlantic.

11
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,320
230 people are aboard. None of them will survive the next 12 minutes.

12
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,160
12 minutes later, at 8 .31 that night,

13
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:41,300
the cockpit voice recorder goes silent.

14
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:45,680
Investigators will later isolate one final fraction of a second on that tape.

15
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:51,220
A loud, explosive sound. Dozens of people on the coast that night

16
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:53,420
will swear, for the rest of their lives,

17
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:56,500
that they saw a streak of light rise up to meet the plane,

18
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:02,160
seconds before it fell. The FBI will spend 16 months and a thousand

19
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:04,040
agents chasing that streak of light.

20
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:09,200
30 years later, the files from that investigation are still being pried loose.

21
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:12,420
One lawsuit, one records request at a time.

22
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:16,320
This is the story of TWA flight 800,

23
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:18,300
the government's official verdict,

24
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:20,380
the men who refused to accept it,

25
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:23,020
and the records that took three decades to surface.

26
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:27,840
To understand why this crash became a 30 -year argument,

27
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:29,560
you have to start with the airline,

28
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:33,000
and the airplane, nobody suspected of anything at all.

29
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:39,020
By the summer of 1996, Transworld Airlines had already filed for bankruptcy

30
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:44,320
twice in four years, a company running on flumes trying to survive one more season.

31
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:50,760
Years earlier, the corporate raider Carl Icahn had walked away from TWA with a ticket

32
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:55,100
resale deal that was still quietly costing the airline real money every single year.

33
00:01:56,060 --> 00:02:01,160
The jet assigned to the Paris run that night was one of the oldest Boeing 747s still

34
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:06,200
flying, 25 years old, with more than 93 ,000 hours already in the

35
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:09,980
air. In the cockpit sat two veteran captains,

36
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:12,800
a flight engineer with three decades at the airline,

37
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,040
and one 25 -year -old trainee named Oliver Crick,

38
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:20,540
26 days into the job, flying only his sixth trip on this aircraft.

39
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:24,040
Behind them sat 212 passengers.

40
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:28,080
16 of them were high school students from a French club in Montoursville,

41
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:31,820
Pennsylvania, on a trip they had spent all year fundraising for.

42
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:34,620
The flight should have left by seven.

43
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:37,760
It didn't push back from the gate until just after eight,

44
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:42,500
held up by a broken piece of ground equipment and a checked bag belonging to a passenger

45
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:45,960
who never boarded. Just days before,

46
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:49,860
TWA had announced its first real quarterly profit in years.

47
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,460
For one summer, it actually looked like the airline might survive.

48
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:57,280
None of that mattered by 8 .19 that night.

49
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:04,060
What happened over the next 12 minutes would take investigators four years and $40 million

50
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:08,800
to fully explain. At 8 .19 p .m.,

51
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:12,920
flight 800 lifted off runway 22R and turned out over the Atlantic,

52
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:16,400
climbing toward cruising altitude for the overnight run to Paris.

53
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:21,820
TWA 800, climb and maintain 15 ,000.

54
00:03:23,180 --> 00:03:25,220
Climb thrust. Climb to 15 ,000.

55
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:30,720
TWA 800. Power set. 37

56
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:32,860
seconds later, the recording stops.

57
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:37,020
Investigators would later isolate one final fraction of a second on that tape,

58
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:42,300
a loud, explosive sound at exactly 8 .31 and 12 seconds.

59
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:45,020
In the next five seconds,

60
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:49,240
the blast tore the cockpit and the first rows of the cabin completely away from the rest

61
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:52,680
of the plane. The rest of the aircraft kept flying,

62
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:57,340
climbing, then falling, trailing fire into the Atlantic Ocean.

63
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:08,730
The wreckage

64
00:04:08,730 --> 00:04:10,570
fell across three separate zones,

65
00:04:10,950 --> 00:04:12,230
stretched along the flight path.

66
00:04:13,150 --> 00:04:16,950
Investigators would spend months matching every single piece back to the exact second

67
00:04:16,950 --> 00:04:20,610
it left the airplane. 230 people were dead,

68
00:04:20,810 --> 00:04:25,410
and within hours, hundreds of witnesses on the ground were already telling investigators

69
00:04:25,410 --> 00:04:27,530
the exact same impossible thing.

70
00:04:28,770 --> 00:04:33,370
The government would spend years explaining what dozens of ordinary people were absolutely

71
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:36,530
certain they had seen. Fishermen,

72
00:04:37,030 --> 00:04:39,570
boaters, a pilot flying nearby.

73
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:41,910
Up and down the Long Island coastline,

74
00:04:42,470 --> 00:04:46,410
dozens of people reported seeing a streak of light rise up from the water toward the

75
00:04:46,410 --> 00:04:48,630
airliner seconds before it exploded.

76
00:04:50,070 --> 00:04:54,610
A commercial pilot flying near the area radioed air traffic control that he had just

77
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:59,530
seen an explosion in the sky and watched something fall burning into the ocean.

78
00:05:00,510 --> 00:05:02,670
To a lot of people who saw it that night,

79
00:05:03,070 --> 00:05:05,730
there was only one explanation that made any sense.

80
00:05:06,430 --> 00:05:11,370
A missile. The FBI opened a criminal investigation within hours,

81
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:13,950
eventually growing to more than a thousand agents.

82
00:05:14,410 --> 00:05:16,050
It would run for 16 months.

83
00:05:17,550 --> 00:05:19,670
The agent in charge, James Colstrom,

84
00:05:20,050 --> 00:05:21,230
would later say there was,

85
00:05:21,510 --> 00:05:25,630
in his own words, not one scintilla of evidence of a bomb or a missile.

86
00:05:26,750 --> 00:05:29,390
No missile debris, no warhead residue,

87
00:05:29,650 --> 00:05:33,650
no radar track showing anything in that sky except Flight 800 alone.

88
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:36,730
The FBI and the safety board both ruled a missile out.

89
00:05:38,130 --> 00:05:42,010
But ruling something out and explaining it to the people who saw it happen with their

90
00:05:42,010 --> 00:05:45,190
own eyes turned out to be two very different problems.

91
00:05:46,670 --> 00:05:50,290
The government's answer to the streak of light came more than a year later,

92
00:05:50,470 --> 00:05:53,890
and it came from an agency nobody expected to see involved.

93
00:05:54,730 --> 00:05:58,750
In November of 1997, at an FBI press conference,

94
00:05:59,170 --> 00:06:01,450
investigators played an animation for the cameras.

95
00:06:01,910 --> 00:06:04,210
It had been produced by the Central Intelligence Agency.

96
00:06:05,830 --> 00:06:10,050
Its explanation, the streak of light wasn't a missile rising toward the plane at all.

97
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:12,690
It was the plane itself, already broken,

98
00:06:13,150 --> 00:06:16,590
already burning, still climbing on momentum before it finally fell.

99
00:06:17,650 --> 00:06:19,870
The explanation did not end the argument.

100
00:06:20,470 --> 00:06:23,630
A member of the FBI's own missile evidence team pushed back on it,

101
00:06:23,850 --> 00:06:25,610
so did an internal CIA memo,

102
00:06:25,990 --> 00:06:29,730
which put the plane's height thousands of feet lower than the animation showed.

103
00:06:31,230 --> 00:06:36,230
Years later, the physicist Tom Stalkup took the same radar data and asked the National

104
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:39,450
Transportation Safety Board to reopen the case entirely.

105
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:43,690
The board reviewed his petition and said no.

106
00:06:44,910 --> 00:06:47,050
The government's story never changed.

107
00:06:47,630 --> 00:06:49,390
But by the time it had finished being told,

108
00:06:49,870 --> 00:06:54,630
five years had passed, and the airplane itself had already told investigators almost

109
00:06:54,630 --> 00:06:56,070
everything they needed to know.

110
00:06:57,390 --> 00:06:59,650
While Washington argued about a streak of light,

111
00:07:00,150 --> 00:07:02,730
the real answer was still sitting on the ocean floor,

112
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:06,390
waiting to be pulled up. Within days,

113
00:07:06,770 --> 00:07:10,930
the largest air and sea recovery operation in aviation history was underway off Long

114
00:07:10,930 --> 00:07:13,150
Island. The Navy, the Coast Guard,

115
00:07:13,470 --> 00:07:16,650
the FBI, 200 divers, more than a dozen ships.

116
00:07:18,030 --> 00:07:22,410
Navy divers worked the debris field in water only about 120 feet deep,

117
00:07:22,870 --> 00:07:25,770
hauling up sections of fuselage one crane lift at a time.

118
00:07:27,350 --> 00:07:30,170
Coast Guard cutters carried the wreckage ashore for cataloguing,

119
00:07:30,290 --> 00:07:31,910
piece by piece, for months.

120
00:07:32,950 --> 00:07:35,250
By the time the operation ended,

121
00:07:35,750 --> 00:07:39,250
investigators had recovered more than 95 % of the aircraft,

122
00:07:39,610 --> 00:07:42,370
and the remains of every single person on board.

123
00:07:43,830 --> 00:07:45,530
At a leased hangar in Calverton,

124
00:07:45,810 --> 00:07:50,550
New York, technicians spent months rebuilding the plane's shattered frame in three dimensions,

125
00:07:51,070 --> 00:07:52,970
piece by piece, rivet by rivet,

126
00:07:52,970 --> 00:07:55,130
chasing the exact point where it had come apart.

127
00:07:55,130 --> 00:07:57,750
It wasn't built for display.

128
00:07:58,310 --> 00:07:59,630
It was a diagnostic tool,

129
00:07:59,970 --> 00:08:02,470
the only way to answer one specific question.

130
00:08:03,170 --> 00:08:06,110
Where, exactly, did this airplane break apart?

131
00:08:07,430 --> 00:08:09,410
To find the spark that started it,

132
00:08:09,870 --> 00:08:11,290
engineers didn't just study wreckage.

133
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:13,310
At a national laboratory,

134
00:08:13,650 --> 00:08:15,790
they built a working copy of the fuel tank,

135
00:08:16,050 --> 00:08:18,810
heated it, vented it, and set it off deliberately,

136
00:08:19,230 --> 00:08:22,670
again and again, trying to recreate one hot summer night.

137
00:08:23,630 --> 00:08:26,710
The answer had been sitting in the wreckage the entire time.

138
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:29,510
It had simply taken four years to read it correctly.

139
00:08:31,070 --> 00:08:32,270
Four years after the crash,

140
00:08:32,710 --> 00:08:35,950
the National Transportation Safety Board finally delivered its verdict.

141
00:08:37,250 --> 00:08:39,770
The center fuel tank, nearly empty that night,

142
00:08:40,030 --> 00:08:41,530
filled mostly with warm vapor,

143
00:08:42,030 --> 00:08:44,010
had exploded. That explosion,

144
00:08:44,310 --> 00:08:47,270
the board concluded, was what tore the aircraft apart.

145
00:08:48,310 --> 00:08:50,050
What actually set it off,

146
00:08:50,450 --> 00:08:52,690
the board could not say with total certainty,

147
00:08:53,150 --> 00:08:55,350
but of every possible spark they tested,

148
00:08:55,790 --> 00:08:57,490
one stood out as the most likely,

149
00:08:57,910 --> 00:09:02,090
a short circuit letting stray voltage leak into the tank through old,

150
00:09:02,530 --> 00:09:07,770
worn wiring. That wiring belonged to the system that told the cockpit how much fuel

151
00:09:07,770 --> 00:09:11,970
was left. On this 747, and on nearly everyone like it,

152
00:09:12,130 --> 00:09:15,230
it ran bundled together with the plane's ordinary power wiring,

153
00:09:15,610 --> 00:09:17,670
with no rule requiring it be kept apart.

154
00:09:19,310 --> 00:09:23,230
When investigators pulled wiring out of 26 other 747s,

155
00:09:23,450 --> 00:09:26,410
every single older aircraft they checked showed the same thing,

156
00:09:26,710 --> 00:09:29,310
chafed, cracked, or contaminated insulation,

157
00:09:29,790 --> 00:09:33,250
decades old. This wasn't one broken airplane.

158
00:09:33,830 --> 00:09:35,630
It was an entire aging fleet,

159
00:09:36,210 --> 00:09:37,590
wired the exact same way,

160
00:09:37,810 --> 00:09:39,150
for the exact same reasons,

161
00:09:39,510 --> 00:09:43,590
for decades. Investigators also found a second,

162
00:09:43,890 --> 00:09:48,670
quite a problem. The fuel tank sat right beside the plane's air conditioning packs,

163
00:09:49,210 --> 00:09:53,030
warm enough for hours at a stretch to fill with flammable vapor.

164
00:09:53,870 --> 00:09:56,110
Nobody had designed the plane with that risk in mind.

165
00:09:57,130 --> 00:10:00,670
The board's own report never claimed total certainty,

166
00:10:00,910 --> 00:10:02,610
and that one honest sentence,

167
00:10:02,990 --> 00:10:04,430
we cannot say for certain,

168
00:10:04,550 --> 00:10:08,690
is the sentence a small number of people would spend the next three decades refusing

169
00:10:08,690 --> 00:10:13,190
to accept. Long before the safety board finished its work,

170
00:10:13,670 --> 00:10:16,770
other people had already decided they knew what really happened.

171
00:10:17,870 --> 00:10:19,990
Before investigators had even finished,

172
00:10:20,390 --> 00:10:24,690
the veteran journalist Pierre Salinger stood up at a press event in France and announced

173
00:10:24,690 --> 00:10:28,730
that Flight 800 had been shot down by a United States Navy missile.

174
00:10:29,270 --> 00:10:31,030
He said he had a document to prove it.

175
00:10:32,470 --> 00:10:35,650
Investigators checked. The document did not hold up.

176
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:38,110
Salinger never produced anything more.

177
00:10:39,370 --> 00:10:42,510
Investigators had a name for what kept happening around this case.

178
00:10:43,090 --> 00:10:44,850
They called it Pierre Salinger Syndrome,

179
00:10:45,430 --> 00:10:46,970
treating an unverified rumor,

180
00:10:47,430 --> 00:10:50,350
repeated enough times, as though it had already been confirmed.

181
00:10:51,710 --> 00:10:53,770
A different journalist, James Sanders,

182
00:10:54,170 --> 00:10:58,690
went further. He and his wife arranged to have two strips of the plane's actual seat

183
00:10:58,690 --> 00:11:00,750
fabric smuggled out for private testing,

184
00:11:01,190 --> 00:11:03,970
convinced a reddish residue on them was missile propellant.

185
00:11:04,950 --> 00:11:08,270
Government labs found the residue was ordinary seat adhesive.

186
00:11:08,750 --> 00:11:13,530
In 1999, both Sanders were convicted of conspiring to steal government property.

187
00:11:14,910 --> 00:11:16,950
The theory found believers in Washington too.

188
00:11:17,410 --> 00:11:21,950
At the time, Senator Alphonse DiMato of New York publicly said he believed a missile

189
00:11:21,950 --> 00:11:25,430
brought the plane down. Two decades later,

190
00:11:25,710 --> 00:11:28,370
the author Jack Cashel was still making the same case,

191
00:11:28,610 --> 00:11:31,310
in a book arguing the missile theory had been covered up,

192
00:11:31,570 --> 00:11:35,910
not disproven. Physicist Tom Stulkup's reopening petition,

193
00:11:36,430 --> 00:11:37,910
the one the safety board rejected,

194
00:11:38,190 --> 00:11:40,910
became one more chapter in the very same argument.

195
00:11:42,310 --> 00:11:45,890
For 25 years, none of it changed the government's official verdict.

196
00:11:46,570 --> 00:11:50,370
Then, in 2022, a new lawsuit reached federal court,

197
00:11:50,830 --> 00:11:54,530
filed by people the government's own investigation had never fully convinced.

198
00:11:55,550 --> 00:11:58,410
This is where the story stops being settled history,

199
00:11:58,850 --> 00:12:02,850
and becomes a case still moving through federal court three decades later.

200
00:12:04,190 --> 00:12:05,570
The lawsuit named Raytheon,

201
00:12:05,970 --> 00:12:10,850
Lockheed Martin, and the federal government itself alleges an errant navy missile test

202
00:12:10,850 --> 00:12:14,750
fired near the crash site that night is what actually brought the plane down.

203
00:12:15,730 --> 00:12:18,770
One of the lead plaintiffs was a man named Ronald Crick.

204
00:12:19,210 --> 00:12:24,230
His son, Oliver, the 25 -year -old flight engineer trainee flying his sixth trip that

205
00:12:24,230 --> 00:12:25,890
night, had died in the crash.

206
00:12:27,510 --> 00:12:31,750
As of this recording, the case is still working its way through a federal appeals court.

207
00:12:32,150 --> 00:12:33,750
Its outcome remains undecided.

208
00:12:34,850 --> 00:12:38,610
In July of 2026, 30 years after the crash,

209
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:41,610
the watchdog group Judicial Watch forced the FBI,

210
00:12:41,930 --> 00:12:43,610
through a freedom of information lawsuit,

211
00:12:44,030 --> 00:12:46,170
to release records it had held back for decades.

212
00:12:47,710 --> 00:12:53,210
Among them, an early FBI teletype stating the bureau had received faxes from Cairo claiming

213
00:12:53,210 --> 00:12:55,430
responsibility for bringing the plane down.

214
00:12:56,070 --> 00:12:58,950
The document does not say who sent those faxes.

215
00:12:59,170 --> 00:13:01,750
It includes no assessment of whether the claim was credible,

216
00:13:02,050 --> 00:13:04,010
and across 16 months of investigating,

217
00:13:04,530 --> 00:13:06,910
the FBI never found anything to back it up.

218
00:13:08,190 --> 00:13:09,730
It wasn't an isolated request.

219
00:13:10,290 --> 00:13:15,370
That same spring, the same watchdog group had filed 13 separate records requests across

220
00:13:15,370 --> 00:13:16,850
eight different federal agencies.

221
00:13:16,850 --> 00:13:19,590
When the Central Intelligence Agency didn't respond,

222
00:13:20,070 --> 00:13:21,670
they sued the agency directly,

223
00:13:21,850 --> 00:13:26,230
over records tied to the very animation Washington had used to explain away the streak

224
00:13:26,230 --> 00:13:28,830
of light. James Cawlstrom,

225
00:13:29,190 --> 00:13:31,490
the FBI agent who led the original investigation,

226
00:13:31,950 --> 00:13:33,850
defended its finding for the rest of his life.

227
00:13:34,430 --> 00:13:37,470
He died in 2021, still certain it was old wiring,

228
00:13:37,730 --> 00:13:39,610
not a missile, that brought the plane down.

229
00:13:40,750 --> 00:13:45,470
That same year, the safety board quietly took apart the reconstructed wreckage it had

230
00:13:45,470 --> 00:13:46,750
kept for a quarter century,

231
00:13:47,310 --> 00:13:49,750
exactly as it had promised the victims' families it would,

232
00:13:49,990 --> 00:13:52,490
once the reconstruction was no longer needed for training.

233
00:13:53,950 --> 00:13:58,570
30 years on, the families of the 230 people aboard that plane remain divided.

234
00:13:59,310 --> 00:14:01,370
Some have made their peace with the government's answer.

235
00:14:01,890 --> 00:14:03,030
Others are still fighting,

236
00:14:03,250 --> 00:14:05,170
in federal court, for a different one.

237
00:14:06,210 --> 00:14:08,630
Machines fail. Old wiring.

238
00:14:09,390 --> 00:14:13,230
Warm fuel vapor. One spark investigators could never fully name.

239
00:14:13,750 --> 00:14:15,910
That part, the record actually explains.

240
00:14:17,450 --> 00:14:19,250
What's harder to explain is why.

241
00:14:19,570 --> 00:14:23,970
Three decades later, families and lawyers are still filing appeals over an answer the

242
00:14:23,970 --> 00:14:25,590
government has long considered closed.

243
00:14:26,590 --> 00:14:31,090
The reconstructed wreckage that once held the real proof does not exist anymore.

244
00:14:31,490 --> 00:14:33,750
It was quietly dismantled in 2021,

245
00:14:34,290 --> 00:14:35,750
its lessons already learned,

246
00:14:36,030 --> 00:14:38,630
its case officially already closed.

247
00:14:40,270 --> 00:14:42,090
